/-!
# Affine objects of a big fppf site have the finite subcovering property

A coproduct of copies of the structure sheaf of a ringed site is computed section by section
over an object all of whose covering sieves contain a finite covering family, and over no other.
This file proves that an affine object of a big fppf site of schemes is such an object, which is
the only place quasi-compactness enters the computation of those sections.

The proof has three steps, and only the middle one is geometric.

*From a sieve to a family.*  The topology of a big fppf site is generated by its selected
covering families, and membership in a generated topology is an inductive closure, not a
statement about families.  But the selected families satisfy the three site axioms, and that is
enough: an induction over the closure produces, for every covering sieve, a selected covering
family all of whose members belong to the sieve.  The isomorphism axiom handles the maximal
sieve, the base change axiom the pullbacks, and the composition axiom the local character.

*Cutting the family down.*  A member of an fppf covering family is flat and locally of finite
presentation, hence an open map, so the images of the members form an open cover of the target.
An affine scheme is quasi-compact, so finitely many of those images already cover, and the
corresponding subfamily is again jointly surjective with flat, finitely presented members.  The
selected collection of a big fppf site is the collection of fppf covering families whose index
type obeys a fixed cardinal bound, and both conditions are inherited by a subfamily, so the
subfamily is again selected.

*Passing upwards.*  Covering sieves of an object of a slice site correspond to covering sieves
of its underlying object, compatibly with the formation of the sieve generated by a family, so
the property transfers from the underlying object to the slice and iterates to slices of slices.
For the site a category fibred in groupoids inherits along its structure functor the transfer is
not formal in the same way, since a covering family upstairs is a family of strongly cartesian
morphisms whose images cover downstairs: a finite family of the base has to be lifted, which
fibredness in groupoids does, and the lifts are again strongly cartesian because every morphism
of a category fibred in groupoids is.  Finiteness is carried along because the lifted family has
the same index type.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.hasFiniteSubcovering_over`: the finite subcovering
  property passes from an object to the slice site above it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.exists_finset_ofArrows_mem_fppfPrecoverage`: **an fppf covering family
  of a quasi-compact scheme has a finite subfamily which is again an fppf covering family.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfSite.exists_finset_isCovering_restrictIndex`: the same for the
  selected covering families of a big fppf site.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfSite.hasFiniteSubcovering`: **a quasi-compact object of a big fppf
  site has the finite subcovering property.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.hasFiniteSubcovering` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.hasFiniteSubcovering_over`: the same for an affine object of the
  big fppf site of a scheme and of a slice of it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.hasFiniteSubcovering_associatedTopology` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.hasFiniteSubcovering_over_associatedTopology`: **an affine
  object of the site a category fibred in groupoids inherits from the big fppf site, and of a
  slice of it, has the finite subcovering property.**

## Implementation notes

The descent from a covering sieve to a selected covering family is
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfSite.exists_isCovering_sieve₀_le` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.Fppf.SieveToFamily`, which runs the induction over the
closure for an arbitrary class of covering families satisfying `CategoryTheory.IsSite`, in the
index universe `CategoryTheory.Covering` uses, so it applies to the selected families of a big
fppf site with no change of shape.

Quasi-compactness is taken as `CompactSpace` on the underlying scheme rather than as
`IsAffine`, since that is all the argument uses; affineness enters only through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.compactSpace_of_isAffine`, and the statements about affine objects
take the affineness of the underlying scheme as an explicit argument because the objects of the
site are subtypes on which no `IsAffine` instance is available.  For a fibre product of affines
that argument is supplied by instance search, through
`AlgebraicGeometry.isAffine_of_isAffine_isAffine_isAffine`, so the hypothesis costs a consumer
working over an affine base nothing at the fibre products of an affine covering.

The members of a selected covering family are cut down inside the selected collection itself
rather than through the standard-covering representation of Stacks 03WX: the selected collection
of a big fppf site is the collection of fppf covering families subject to a cardinal bound on
the index type, and a subfamily inherits both halves of that conjunction.  So no passage to a
tautologically equivalent standard covering is needed.

The finite subfamily is produced as a `Finset` of the index type and converted to an
`Fin`-indexed family through `Finset.equivFin`; the two generate the same presieve by
`CategoryTheory.PreZeroHypercover.presieve₀_restrictIndex_equiv`, so no reindexing lemma of its
own is needed.

For the inherited site no induction over the closure has to be run by hand: the inherited
collection of covering families satisfies the closure properties of a precoverage whenever the
base collection does, which the repeated instances of
`CategoryTheory.Functor.inheritedPrecoverage` supply, so
`CategoryTheory.Precoverage.mem_toGrothendieck_iff_of_isStableUnderComposition` already
identifies the covering sieves as those containing a covering family.  Only the fppf topology is
treated, as in `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.coversTop_affineObj`: the site axioms are available
for the selected fppf families and not for the four coarser selected collections.

